{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/probability-mass-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/probability-mass-function.html","questions":[{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the probability mass function of a discrete random variable $X$?","options":["The function $p_X(x)=P(X=x)$","The function $F_X(x)=P(X\\le x)$ only","The support of $X$ without probabilities","The event $X\\in S$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f7df3e0d32e89797ea9a76bb"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The CDF $F_X(x)=P(X\\le x)$ of a discrete variable is obtained from its PMF by:","options":["differentiating the PMF","integrating the PMF","taking the largest mass","summing $p_X(t)$ over all $t\\le x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8e2800fc91110e2eb267d43e"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The support of a discrete distribution is the set of values $x$ where:","options":["$p_X(x)>0$","$p_X(x)=0$","$p_X(x)=1$","$x$ is an integer"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4dfdce6bc79e895d0fc49e80"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What two basic properties must a PMF satisfy?","options":["It is nonnegative and its masses sum to one","It is decreasing and sums to zero","It is negative outside the support and sums to two","It is right-continuous and has no masses"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3f3eceafc1637fb45c842d5d"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How are event probabilities computed from a PMF?","options":["By multiplying all possible values","By summing the masses of values inside the event","By subtracting the support from one, no matter which values are selected.","By ignoring individual values"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0426976d913a42d8ce0b4482"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a fair die, what PMF value is assigned to each value $1$ through $6$?","options":["$1$","$0$","$6$","$1/6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"14a3cc7c9b0e505efcbd5c0b"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a Bernoulli variable, where does the PMF assign mass?","options":["At $0$ and $1$","At every real number","Only at negative integers","Only at $365$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4c2cd5645a7f9f4b8a39fb00"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a Poisson variable, where does the PMF assign mass?","options":["At every real number in $[0,1]$","Only at $0$ and $1$","At every nonnegative integer","Only at six die faces"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b05dbf61a237b6c9128c8a5a"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $p_X(2)=0.3$, what does this mean?","options":["$P(X\\le2)=0.3$ always","$P(X=2)=0.3$","$P(X>2)=0.3$ always","$X$ cannot equal $2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6fd971584fef6d43fd52183b"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If a PMF has masses $0.2$, $0.3$, and $0.5$ on three values, do the masses sum to one?","options":["Yes","No, they sum to $0.9$","No, they sum to $2$","Only if one mass is negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3da18df400b5694a628d8934"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a discrete variable, $E[X]$ is computed from the PMF as:","options":["$\\int x\\,p_X(x)\\,dx$","$\\sum_x x\\,p_X(x)$","$\\sum_x p_X(x)$","$\\max_x p_X(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"315ac3ce889c0ff9826fc77f"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must PMF values be nonnegative?","options":["They are densities that must be negative","They are probabilities of individual-value events","They are sample-space labels","They are factorial counts"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba638c0448e573b1414180e1"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the difference between a PMF and a CDF?","options":["A PMF is always continuous; a CDF is never a function","A PMF is for sample spaces only; a CDF is for events only, under all circumstances whatsoever.","A PMF gives point probabilities; a CDF gives accumulated probabilities $P(X\\le x)$","They are exactly the same notation"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c86d42efa0f93570f4f5b4d"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $p_X(0)=0.4$ and $p_X(1)=0.6$, what is $P(X\\in\\{0,1\\})$?","options":["$0.2$","$1.0$","$0.4$","$0.6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a1d65c816b8860cac9c13512"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For a fair die, what is $P(X\\in\\{2,4,6\\})$ using the PMF?","options":["$1/6$","$6/3$","$0$","$3/6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7cab529481da627b5ec3d5f8"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If a proposed PMF has masses $0.5$ and $0.7$ on two values only, what is wrong?","options":["The masses sum to $1.2$, not one","The masses are nonnegative","There are two values","PMFs cannot have finite support"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"50283753bc3dc8008a652677"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is a PMF not appropriate as the main description for a purely continuous variable?","options":["Continuous variables generally do not allocate positive probability to individual values","Continuous variables have no CDF","PMFs can only take negative values","Continuous variables cannot be random variables, for every possible choice of input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dba2f2c2bdac24a555868465"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a zero PMF value at $x$ indicate for a discrete variable?","options":["The value $x$ must occur with probability one","The value $x$ has no probability mass","The whole PMF is invalid","The CDF must decrease at $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e319443485260fc266b4066b"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A discrete variable has masses $p(0)=0.2$, $p(1)=0.5$, $p(2)=0.3$. What is $P(X\\ge1)$?","options":["$0.8$","$0.5$","$0.2$","$1.0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a71711467314deecccec650d"},{"id":"pt_batch5_probability_mass_function_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A PMF on $\\{0,1,2\\}$ has $p(0)=0.25$ and $p(1)=0.25$. What must $p(2)$ be?","options":["$0.50$","$0.25$","$1.50$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2fc7c4dfa57704aaa3ab0029"}]}}